title The promise no tool could read
prompt Autonomous loop -- roster cold first, Standfast on reds, next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, rota row N mod 5, condense ITINERARY and refill Now, send each finished increment
think Lap index from git rev-list --count HEAD = 3372, so the council rota reads row 3372 mod 5 = 2 -- Fire and Mars, cut and stop. Standfast, reds-first, and the three depths of removal. Standfast governed the lap directly: the cold roster opened RED and the line stopped there before any new work began.
think The cold roster read 49 guards, 48 green, 1 red -- vocabulary_collection refusing construction/ITINERARY.md. The red was in HEAD rather than in my working tree. Root: the previous lap wrote its Git nib line by quoting its own session log, and a session log is exempt from that guard while the operator card is on its enforce roster. An exemption belongs to a room, not to a sentence. Repaired the word, guard GREEN on metal, booked as REDS %196.
think The named crux from the last two recommends was docs/CRYPTO.md at 69,099 bytes, and the ITINERARY said it wants the same split its sibling took. Measuring said otherwise. Every one of the 80 .rye modules the page names already carries a row in crypto/MODULES.md, which holds all 87 -- so the page's rung table is a subset copy of a record that already stands complete beside the code. A split would have moved that copy to a second file; compressing removes it.
think The docs shelf's own law, written at the top of docs/README.md, already said this: pages compress, hold no load-bearing pins, and leave the raw record beneath them. A 44,306-byte Compositions section on a compression shelf is the opposite of the shelf's own rule, and the shelf had been carrying it.
think The second open reading was the Ceiling: header nobody measures. Measured for the first time: 13 pages declare one, and NONE was both readable and honoured. Twelve wrote the single-glyph form of <= that no parser here reaches; the thirteenth had its header rewritten into prose narrating its own drift. Underneath the unreadable declarations two pages stood over their number -- docs/README.md 43 against 40, docs/CRYPTO.md 311 against 300.
think That is the finding rather than the count: a ceiling gate alone would have read this shelf clean, because a declaration it cannot parse is a page it never measures. So the readable-form gate is not tidiness riding along beside the real check -- it is what makes the real check reach anything. Both gate at zero.
think A third declaration form exists and I nearly shipped a guard blind to it. docs/STOA.md writes <=80 into its H1 title in the shelf's grade notation and stands at 166 lines. Gating it would force a page split into this round, so it rides a ceiling that only falls and prints every roster pass. A guard reading 13 of 14 declarations in silence is REDS %187's shape, and this arc has booked that three times.
obs The scan builds the glyph it discourages from octal bytes rather than embedding it, so the guard stays plain ASCII itself. A guard written in the character it discourages would teach one thing and do another.
obs docs/STOA.md stood on disk and in no index -- the shelf's front door listed 14 of its 15 pages. Listed now, and the recited grade column retired: a second copy of each page's bound is a bound that can quietly disagree with itself, and the new guard reads the page's own header.
obs docs/README.md came in at 45 lines against the 40 it declares, so I trimmed rather than moving the number. A guard whose ceiling you raise to pass it is a guard with a door beside it.
obs The card's Git nib line held 1,009 bytes of pasted log abstract. Condensed to 267 -- the card names the nib and one sentence, since an account of a lap belongs in its log.
obs Booked honestly rather than swept: this card reads 47,188 bytes against the 24,576 bound, condensed to 41,449 four laps ago and grown back. Named in Next as its own round.
loom witness=declared_ceiling_control behaviors=13 assertions=19 refusals_bitten=5 wall_s=2
